</span><span>&nbsp;</span><span style="font-weight:700">Vin&iacute;cius J&uacute;nior</span><span>, renewed to 2032. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Endrick</span><span>, retained after Roma and Aston Villa approaches; Mourinho has said publicly he is going nowhere. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Aur&eacute;lien Tchouam&eacute;ni</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">, after a new deal.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;height:11pt;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal"></span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">THE HOLE &mdash;</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;a controlling No. 6. Rodri was the plan. Rodri now looks like Barcelona&rsquo;s.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Sources: Real Madrid official communiqu&eacute;s (6 Aug); Eurosport; Football365; Sky Sports; Fabrizio Romano. Fees marked &ldquo;reported&rdquo; are press figures &mdash; the club has disclosed none.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#0f7a4e;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">TRACKER &middot; THE MONEY BOARD</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Ten biggest confirmed fees of the summer 2026 window, in &pound;m. Chart built by this paper from Football365&rsquo;s window ledger, cross-checked against club announcements.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Morgan Rogers</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;&middot; Villa &rarr; Chelsea</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Elliot Anderson</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;&middot; Forest &rarr; Man City</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Yan Diom&aacute;nde</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;&middot; Leipzig &rarr; Real Madrid</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Sandro Tonali</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;&middot; Newcastle &rarr; Tottenham</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Mateus Fernandes</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;&middot; West Ham &rarr; Tottenham</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Bruno Guimar&atilde;es</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;&middot; Newcastle &rarr; Arsenal</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Anthony Gordon</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;&middot; Newcastle &rarr; Barcelona</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Gon&ccedil;alo Ramos</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;&middot; PSG &rarr; AC Milan</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Crysencio Summerville</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;&middot; West Ham &rarr; Al Hilal</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">J&eacute;r&eacute;my Jacquet</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;&middot; Rennes &rarr; Liverpool</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Green bar = deal completed inside this edition&rsquo;s reporting window (5&ndash;12 August). Sterling conversions are the reporting outlet&rsquo;s; several of these fees are undisclosed by the clubs involved.</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Manchester City named their price. Barcelona have until Friday.</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">A &euro;20m gap, a report-back date and a &pound;120m domino sitting behind it. The Rodri auction is the most consequential unresolved deal in Europe.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The sequence, as far as it can be established: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Barcelona</span><span>&rsquo;s opening bid &mdash; reported by </span><span style="font-weight:700">Sky Sports</span><span>&nbsp;at around &pound;40m, or &euro;45m plus &euro;5m in variables &mdash; was rejected by </span><span style="font-weight:700">Manchester City</span><span>&nbsp;on Friday 7 August as, in the words of the report, &ldquo;far too low.&rdquo; City want upwards of &pound;60m; the Spanish press has their ask at &euro;70m (</span><span style="font-weight:700">Depor</span><span>) or &euro;80m (</span><span style="font-weight:700">El Espa&ntilde;ol</span><span>). By 11 August Barcelona had reportedly climbed to &euro;67&ndash;68m and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Romano</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;had the deal &ldquo;advancing very well.&rdquo; Nothing is agreed.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>Two hard constraints give the thing a shape. The first is the contract: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Rodri</span><span>&nbsp;is into the final year of his City deal, which expires in June 2027, and City would in principle rather renew than sell &mdash; a position </span><span style="font-weight:700">Jamie Carragher</span><span>&nbsp;put bluntly on Sky: </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;If you&rsquo;re Manchester City, what&rsquo;s the point in selling them? You might as well just keep them for the extra year.&rdquo;</span><span>&nbsp;The second is the calendar. Rodri is required to report back to City by </span><span style="font-weight:700">Friday 14 August</span><span>; </span><span style="font-weight:700">Enzo Maresca</span><span>, in his first summer as City&rsquo;s head coach after succeeding </span><span style="font-weight:700">Pep Guardiola</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">, said this week that &ldquo;it seems he will be in Manchester on Wednesday.&rdquo; A player training at Carrington is a player with less leverage than a player refusing to.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>Behind it sits the domino that would make this the story of the window rather than merely a story in it. City&rsquo;s stated preference to replace Rodri is </span><span style="font-weight:700">Enzo Fern&aacute;ndez</span><span>, and here the reporting is genuinely irreconcilable. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Romano</span><span>, on 12 August, has contact made through the agent </span><span style="font-weight:700">Javier Pastore</span><span>, with </span><span style="font-weight:700">Chelsea</span><span>&nbsp;holding at &pound;120m and City &ldquo;not expected to spend this money.&rdquo; </span><span style="font-weight:700">CaughtOffside</span><span>&nbsp;the same day has City &ldquo;confident&rdquo; at &pound;100m with no formal bid lodged. </span><span style="font-weight:700">The Athletic</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">, on 11 August, reports Chelsea now intend to keep him. This paper is not going to pick one. What is safe is that City are not moving for a midfielder until Rodri&rsquo;s position resolves, and that every hour Barcelona spend closing a &euro;15m gap is an hour Chelsea spend deciding whether they want &pound;120m more than they want a No. 8.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">For Barcelona the money has to come from somewhere, and it is coming from the squad &mdash; see the transfer pages below. For Madrid, watching the Golden Ball winner walk to their rivals having just spent &euro;130m on a nineteen-year-old winger, this is the week&rsquo;s quiet humiliation.</span></p><p style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;padding-left:0;font-size:11pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#0f7a4e;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">TRANSFER NEWS</span></p><h3 style="padding-top:0pt;margin:0;color:#000000;font-weight:700;padding-left:0;font-size:14pt;padding-bottom:12pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left;padding-right:0"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:14pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">Newcastle sell the captain, hire the wunderkind, and start again</span></h3><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:italic">Three first-choice players gone for roughly &pound;236m, a 38-year-old head coach in the dugout, and a recruitment plan built on volume. This is the most radical thing any big club in England is doing.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span style="font-weight:700">Bruno Guimar&atilde;es</span><span>, </span><span style="font-weight:700">Newcastle United</span><span>&rsquo;s captain, completed a move on </span><span style="font-weight:700">Friday 8 August</span><span>&nbsp;for a fee reported at around &pound;75m (&euro;87.5m), on a four-year contract with a further one-year option. It is the third major sale of a brutal summer on Tyneside: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Sandro Tonali</span><span>&nbsp;went to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Tottenham</span><span>&nbsp;on 6 July for a fee variously given as &pound;92.5m (&pound;85m plus &pound;7.5m) and &pound;100m &mdash; the outlets have not reconciled &mdash; and </span><span style="font-weight:700">Anthony Gordon</span><span>&nbsp;to </span><span style="font-weight:700">Barcelona</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;for around &pound;69m. Add them up and Newcastle have banked something in the region of &pound;236m from three players in five weeks.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>They have also changed manager. </span><span style="font-weight:700">Eddie Howe</span><span>&nbsp;departed around 30 July after nearly five years, having delivered the club&rsquo;s first major trophy in seventy years with the 2025 League Cup. His replacement, announced on </span><span style="font-weight:700">5 August</span><span>, is </span><span style="font-weight:700">Matthias Jaissle</span><span>&nbsp;&mdash; 38 years old, two Austrian titles at </span><span style="font-weight:700">RB Salzburg</span><span>, back-to-back AFC Champions League Elite titles at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Al Ahli</span><span>. Newcastle did not disclose the contract length. </span><span style="font-style:italic">&ldquo;It is an incredible honour to become head coach of Newcastle United. This is one of the biggest clubs in European football,&rdquo;</span><span>&nbsp;Jaissle said, adding that he &ldquo;fully and unreservedly&rdquo; believes in the project. Sporting director </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ross Wilson</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;framed it in system terms: Jaissle&rsquo;s &ldquo;leadership style and football philosophy are strongly aligned with our sporting vision.&rdquo;</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>The reinvestment is deliberately unglamorous and deliberately young: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Bazoumana Tour&eacute;</span><span>&nbsp;from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Hoffenheim</span><span>&nbsp;(reported c.&euro;49m), </span><span style="font-weight:700">Sean Steur</span><span>&nbsp;from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Ajax</span><span>&nbsp;(c.&euro;27m), </span><span style="font-weight:700">Luk&aacute;&scaron; Horn&iacute;&ccedil;ek</span><span>&nbsp;from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Braga</span><span>&nbsp;(&pound;25.7m, 3 Aug) and </span><span style="font-weight:700">James Trafford</span><span>&nbsp;from </span><span style="font-weight:700">Manchester City</span><span>. Reporting on Tyneside points to six to eight arrivals in total. The live thread is </span><span style="font-weight:700">Amar Dedi&#263;</span><span>, the 23-year-old Bosnia right-back at </span><span style="font-weight:700">Benfica</span><span>&nbsp;who played under Jaissle at Salzburg: </span><span style="font-weight:700">Romano</span><span style="color:#000000;font-weight:400;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;font-style:normal">&nbsp;reported club-to-club talks on 11 August with the player keen and no fee disclosed. Status: concrete interest.</span></p><p style="padding:0;margin:0;color:#000000;font-size:11pt;font-family:&quot;Arial&quot;;line-height:1.0;text-align:left"><span>What Newcastle are not doing is selling any more. They rejected an approach for </span><span style="font-weight:700">Lewis Hall</span><span>&nbsp;reported at &pound;60m from </span><span style="font-weight:700">M